---
/**
 * /test/controller/ps-calibration — PS Controller Calibration Tool
 * Master SEO & Technical Content Optimization
 * Primary keyword: ps5 controller calibration
 */

import ToolLayout from '@layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import PSCalibration from '@components/tools/controller/PSCalibration.astro';

const breadcrumbs = [
  { name: 'Tests', url: '/test/controller' },
  { name: 'Controller Tests', url: '/test/controller' },
  { name: 'PS Controller Calibration' },
];

const faqs = [
  {
    question: 'How do I check whether my PS5 or PS4 controller needs calibrating?',
    answer: 'Connect the controller, press a button, let go of both sticks and press "Sample Center". The tool reports each stick\'s resting offset as a percentage. Up to about 2.5% is normal wear; higher values mean games see the stick as slightly pushed even when you are not touching it.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Does this page recalibrate my controller?',
    answer: 'No. It reads the sticks through the browser\'s Gamepad API and changes nothing on the controller. It tells you whether calibration (or cleaning or replacing the stick) is worth doing, and lets you check the result afterwards.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How do I actually recalibrate DualSense or DualShock 4 sticks?',
    answer: 'Sony offers no official recalibration tool for the DualSense or DualShock 4. The open-source community utility dualshock-tools sends the controller\'s own calibration commands over WebHID from Chrome or Edge. It is third-party software, so read its instructions first, use a USB cable, and re-test here afterwards.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Will calibration fix stick drift?',
    answer: 'Only partly. Recalibration re-centers a stick whose resting point has shifted slightly, which is common after a Hall-effect module swap. A worn potentiometer keeps getting worse, so if the resting offset climbs again within weeks, the module needs cleaning or replacing.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What does the outer range check measure?',
    answer: 'While you roll the sticks around their edge for 8 seconds, it counts how many of 36 ten-degree directions reach at least 90% deflection. A healthy stick covers nearly all of them; gaps point to debris or a worn gate limiting travel.',
  },
];
---

<ToolLayout
  title="PS5 & PS4 Controller Calibration Check | CT"
  description="Measure your DualSense or DualShock 4 stick center offset and outer range in the browser, then learn when and how recalibration actually helps."
  keywords="ps5 controller calibration, dualsense calibration check, ps4 controller calibration, dualsense stick center offset"
  toolName="PS5 / PS4 Stick Calibration Check"
  category="Controller Tests"
  categoryUrl="/test/controller"
  breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs}
  faqs={faqs}
>
  {/* ── Tool Interactive Island ── */}
  <Fragment slot="tool">
    <PSCalibration />
  </Fragment>

  {/* ── Results Slot ── */}
  <Fragment slot="results">
    <div class="results-summary">
      <h2 class="section-title">Reading your result</h2>
      <div class="results-legend-grid">
        <div class="legend-card legend-pass">
          <strong class="legend-title">0 – 2.5% resting offset</strong>
          <p class="legend-text">Normal. A game deadzone just above this value hides it without costing aim precision.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="legend-card legend-warning">
          <strong class="legend-title">2.5 – 8%</strong>
          <p class="legend-text">Noticeable. Recalibration can help if the stick was recently replaced; otherwise clean the module and re-test.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="legend-card legend-fail">
          <strong class="legend-title">Above 8%</strong>
          <p class="legend-text">Heavy offset. Usually a worn potentiometer. Calibration will not hold for long; plan a module replacement.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </Fragment>

  {/* ── Export Slot ── */}
  <Fragment slot="export">
    <div class="export-banner">
      <div>
        <h3 class="export-title">Watch the drift live</h3>
        <p class="export-desc">The Stick Drift test plots both sticks in real time, which helps after cleaning or recalibrating.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="export-buttons">
        <a href="/test/controller/drift" class="btn-primary">Test Stick Drift</a>
        <a href="/fix/stick-drift/ps5-dualsense" class="btn-secondary">DualSense Repair Guide</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </Fragment>

  {/* ── Related Tools Slot ── */}
  <Fragment slot="related-tools">
    <a href="/test/controller/drift" class="tool-card card-base card-hover">
      <h3 class="tool-name">Stick Drift Tester</h3>
      <p class="tool-desc">Live resting-offset plot for both sticks.</p>
    </a>
    <a href="/test/controller/ps5" class="tool-card card-base card-hover">
      <h3 class="tool-name">PS5 Controller Tester</h3>
      <p class="tool-desc">Buttons, triggers, sticks and vibration in one view.</p>
    </a>
    <a href="/test/controller/circularity" class="tool-card card-base card-hover">
      <h3 class="tool-name">Circularity Test</h3>
      <p class="tool-desc">Measure stick range of motion & perimeter error.</p>
    </a>
  </Fragment>

  {/* ── SEO Content Slot ── */}
  <Fragment slot="content">
    <h2>What "calibrating" a PlayStation controller means</h2>
    <p>
      Each stick reports two positions, X and Y. The controller stores a center point and an outer range for each
      stick and maps the raw reading onto them. When a stick wears or a new module is fitted, the real resting point
      drifts away from the stored center, and games see a small push you never made. Recalibration stores a new
      center and range; it does not repair a worn part.
    </p>

    <h3>Measure first</h3>
    <p>
      This page measures the two things calibration changes: the resting offset (step 2) and whether each stick
      reaches full deflection all the way around (step 3). It reads the sticks through the browser's Gamepad API in
      any modern browser, over USB or Bluetooth, and writes nothing to the controller.
    </p>

    <h3>When recalibration is worth it</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>After fitting Hall-effect or replacement sticks:</strong> new modules rarely rest exactly where the old center was stored, so an offset of several percent is expected until you recalibrate.</li>
      <li><strong>A stable, small offset:</strong> if the resting value is the same every time you test, re-centering removes it.</li>
      <li><strong>Not for a worsening stick:</strong> if the offset grows from week to week, the potentiometer is wearing out. Clean or replace it; see the <a href="/fix/stick-drift/ps5-dualsense" class="content-link">DualSense drift repair guide</a>.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>How to recalibrate</h2>
    <p>
      Sony does not publish a recalibration tool. The open-source <a href="https://dualshock-tools.github.io/" rel="noopener" target="_blank" class="content-link font-semibold">dualshock-tools</a>
      utility, maintained by the r/consolerepair community, sends the controller's built-in calibration commands over
      WebHID in Chrome or Edge with a USB cable. It is third-party software; follow its instructions, then come back
      and run steps 2 and 3 again to confirm the offset dropped.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>

<style>
  .results-summary { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 0.5rem; }
  .results-text { font-size: 1rem; color: var(--color-text-secondary); line-height: 1.6; }
  .results-legend-grid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(250px, 1fr)); gap: 1rem; margin-top: 1rem; }
  .legend-card { padding: 1rem; border-radius: 8px; background: var(--color-bg-subtle); border-left: 4px solid var(--color-accent); }
  .export-banner { display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: space-between; gap: 1.5rem; flex-wrap: wrap; }
  .export-title { font-size: 1.125rem; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 0.25rem; }
  .export-desc { font-size: var(--text-small); color: var(--color-text-secondary); }
  .prose { line-height: 1.7; color: var(--color-text-secondary); }
  .prose h2, .prose h3 { color: var(--color-text-primary); margin-top: 1.5rem; margin-bottom: 0.75rem; }
  .prose p { margin-bottom: 1rem; }
  .content-link { color: var(--color-accent); text-decoration: underline; }
</style>
